# Versioning a system dynamics model

A checklist, not a narrative. For the worked example this is derived from, see
the nfdi4sd repository.

## Why this needs its own document

A model file is text, but its diff is not reviewable by default. In the nfdi4sd
example model, adding parameters to a structure-only Vensim model produced a
diff of roughly 655 lines -- about 250 of which were the sketch section, i.e.
the coordinates of boxes on the diagram canvas. Nothing about that third of the
diff carries meaning.

That is one reason for the layout: once parameters live in `models/config/`,
a scenario is a change to the inputs and the model file is not touched at all.

## Configure Vensim before the first commit

**Model → Settings → File Format.** Without these, Vensim writes changes into
the model file that nobody made. The model has to be saved in text (`.mdl`)
format for any of this to matter -- a binary model has nothing to diff.

| Setting | Value | What it prevents |
|---|---|---|
| Equation order | Alphabetical by group | Vensim otherwise reorders equations arbitrarily, so the diff shows reshuffling instead of content |
| Equation format | Verbose | Expanded equations, so a change is actually visible in the diff |
| Model settings | Store user specific settings in a separate file | Keeps personal settings out of the shared model file |
| Override sketch zoom | Autofit to screen | Stops zoom level and viewport from being written into the model |
| Disable sketch recenter on edit | ticked | Vensim otherwise recentres the view while editing and records that |

Source: <https://vensim.com/documentation/svn_git.html>

The third row matters most. Without it, model files accumulate session state --
which runs happen to be loaded in the window, where the view was scrolled to --
and everyone who opens the model produces a diff without touching it. With it,
that state moves to a separate file, which belongs in `.gitignore`.

The last two rows attack the layout noise described above directly: much of it
comes from scrolling and zooming, not from modelling.

## Suggested commit sequence

Each step is a reviewable unit. Tag them so people can `git checkout` any stage.

| Commit | Content | Tag |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Skeleton: directories, README, CITATION.cff, LICENSE, **.gitignore** | `v0.1-scaffold` |
| 2 | Model structure, no parameters | `v0.2-structure` |
| 3 | Parameterised model, simulation settings pinned | `v0.3-parameterized` |
| 4 | Parameters moved to `models/config/` **plus the runner that reads them** | `v0.4-config-external` |
| 5 | A scenario -- one small file | `v0.5-scenario` |

Notes on the steps that are easy to get wrong:

**Commit 1 -- `.gitignore` belongs here, not later.** Modelling tools write
session state into model files (loaded run names, window positions). Without
`.gitignore` and `.gitattributes` from the start, people commit diffs they did
not cause. `.gitattributes` in particular must be in the first commit:
introduced later, `* text=auto` rewrites every file at once.

**Commit 2 is deliberately not runnable.** Structure without parameters computes
nothing. This breaks the usual rule that every commit is a working state. That
is the right trade-off here -- the history documents model development, not a
green main branch -- but say so in the README so it is not mistaken for sloppiness.

**Commit 4 needs a consumer.** Moving parameters into a CSV changes nothing
unless something reads it. Ship `scripts/csv_to_cin.py` (or the equivalent for
your tool) in the same commit.

**Commit 5 is the payoff.** A scenario is a different set of inputs to the same
structure, and the commit should show exactly that. The check is not how small
the diff is -- editing one value inside the model file is small too -- but which
file it touches: `git diff <previous> <this> -- <your model file>` must come
back empty. Then every scenario provably runs the same structure, and any
difference in the results comes from the inputs.

Resist putting it on a branch just to cover branching. A branch isolates work
from a stable state, allows parallel work, or holds something back for review;
adding one small file does none of those, and the merge commit is noise around
the one thing the commit is meant to show.

Branch when a scenario is exploratory and may be discarded, when two people work
in parallel, or when a scenario needs model changes as well. The discardable
case is the useful one to demonstrate:

```bash
git switch -c scenario/probe
# try something: change a parameter, run it, look at the plot
git switch main && git branch -D scenario/probe   # gone; the history never knew
```

An experiment does not have to survive in the history to have been worth
running.

## Which results belong in the repository

**Working output → the binary part stays out.** The modelling tool's own run
files are git-ignored: not diffable, rewritten on every save, unreadable without
a licence for the tool that wrote them. The text and image output in `results/`
is kept -- it costs little and saves a reader from re-running the model to see
what came out. That convenience is not a substitute for reproducibility: if
something in `results/` cannot be re-created from `models/` + `models/config/` +
`runners/`, the repository is missing something -- that is the bug, not the
missing file.

**Reference results → committed**, in a `reference/` directory. A small,
curated set at coarse resolution, updated deliberately. It is what lets someone
without a licence for your modelling tool check your numbers at all, and it is
the input your regression tests compare against. Wire CI to regenerate and
compare, so a stale reference fails the build instead of rotting quietly.

**Results behind a publication → an archived deposit with a DOI.** Not because
a commit hash is a poor identifier -- it is the basis of Software Heritage's
SWHID, an intrinsic identifier standardised as ISO/IEC 18670. What it lacks is
resolution and preservation: it names the exact tree but resolves only while
someone still serves the repository. A DOI supplies resolution and an archival
commitment, and says nothing about content integrity. Use both: tag the release,
archive it, cite the version DOI, record the commit.
